In this test, I try to understand an arraylist. But it is throwing an assertion error "Assert expected true but got false". I see that it does not consider index >= 0 and index < Count conditions in the line AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);. I even tried to add a dummy line of code before the test and it ignored my dummy debugging output. Any thoughts?

A: The first problem is that the ArrayList is not serializable which means it cannot be stored and reused in PEX tests.
So, you'll need to generate a proper ArrayList for each test case, and use the AssumePrecondition method to restrict the values of index to those allowed by Count.
Also, I suspect that Count property could be a problem with testing, because we can't guarantee the actual count in the context of a list where Pex might modify it.
Your test case is designed in a way that only checks the behavior if the index is inside the current bounds of the list, which seems good. You should also add boundary checks (like Count of 0) in your assumptions.
For more complex types, you might have to use a different collection that supports PEX. I suggest you to look at List<T> and consider if it provides enough coverage for your purpose.
Hope this helps you to address your issue.

A: I've modified your test to this:
public void PUT_GetContractTest([PexAssumeValid]List<object> list, [PexRange(0, 5)]int index)
{
    Assume.True(index < list.Count);

    object expected = list[index];
    list.RemoveAt(index);
    bool containsAfterRemove = list.Contains(expected);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_list_Count", list.Count);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$index", index);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$containsAfterRemove", containsAfterRemove);

    if (list.Count == 0) {
        AssumePrecondition.IsFalse(containsAfterRemove);
    }
    else {
        AssumePrecondition.AreEqual(false, containsAfterRemove);
    }
}

First, I've replaced ArrayList with List<object>. Objects can hold any type of values in .NET, so it's a flexible way to work with lists.
Then, I used PexRange attribute to limit the index values to 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5 and changed the assumptions around index to better match the list manipulation done in the test.
Then, I've modified the "$containsAfterRemove" assertion so it matches the expected behavior based upon the removal of the element at the specified index.
I hope this helps solve the problem you're having. 
If this doesn't fix the problem, please provide additional details or code related to the test failure, if possible.
This test is designed to mimic the behavior you're attempting to observe, however PEX may not be able to correctly test the operation of the ArrayList class because it may fail due to the non-serializable ArrayList class.
I'd suggest using the List<T> class if possible to test your code.
Also, always remember to use Assume to restrict your test cases to expected valid scenarios.
