I have the ArrayListContractTest.cstest file in my project. It tells me that "PUT_GetContract" was not instrumented and had unchecked execution. I can't seem to figure out why that would be. I have run this file in my project and it had good coverage before, but now I have manually added the coverage data, and it's still not instrumented. I can't find anything online that helped or if there is something about my test that could be causing this. Any help would be appreciated.

A: I believe the problem here is that the given method PUT_GetContract is not public or internal, nor it is marked with the [PexMethod] attribute.
The issue of the PUT_Contract method not being instrumented is because these criteria are needed for the method to be picked up by Pex for the coverage criteria to be evaluated. 
Pex searches for public or internal methods [not static] and that have been marked by attributes such as [PexMethod] or [PexAssembly] to instrument code. 
If PUT_GetContract method is marked with [PexMethod] attribute or is made public or internal then it should work fine. If not then there can be other reasons for this behavior.
For example:
- the assembly containing your test is not referenced from the test project.
- the test doesn't run after the code coverage has been gathered. 

So make sure you've marked your test method with [PexMethod] attribute, your code with [PexAssembly] if you want to include all types in your code coverage.

A: I can see that you have mentioned some PexObserve and Debug.Assert line, but it seems incomplete, these will be helpful for further analysis on the failing test case. If this all doesn't work, then perhaps the problem might be something else - so I would recommend to use a combination of Debug and System.Diagnostics.Trace for tracing your program execution.
Can you try running the Pex on a reduced set of code, just to make sure that it is not a issue of testing just one method itself?
Also, try instrumenting an equivalent method in another project that is working fine. This could give you a clue about the type of errors that may cause it to fail.
One more thing, since you mentioned about coverage having been added manually, it could be that Pex hasn't been run after this addition for a while, and the coverage might be outdated. Can you try a clean build before continuing?
If all fails, I'd recommend filing a bug with MS on codeplex, including the failing unit test with the failing method, the source code and any error messages.
