## 3.1.6 自动生成测试用例

Pex 提供了各种方法来生成测试用例。在测试用例开发过程中，当我们不知道我们想要测试什么函数或类的具体参数时，我们应该自动生成测试用例。这可以在测试覆盖率的角度提供很大的帮助。

Pex 提供了方法来在测试某个类的所有方法、特定方法或全部类上生成测试用例。

当我们选择特定方法或者全部方法作为测试目标时，我们应该提供一些假设来指导 Pex 生成测试用例。

例如我们有一个 MathUtil 类的 Math 方法，我们想要生成一些测试用例，那么我们可以通过提供一些假设来指导 Pex，假设 Math 方法的第一个参数应为正数且小于等于 100，第二个参数也应为正数且小于等于 100，然后 Pex 就会自动生成一系列的用例。

这种方法可以大大减少我们需要手动创建测试用例的数量，并且可以更准确地测试到我们意想不到的边界情况。

## 3.1.7 观察返回的值

在 Pex 中我们也可以观察返回的值，这对于我们确定我们编写的测试是否正确非常有用。Pex 允许我们在控制台中查看返回的值，并且还可以通过设置断点来观察返回的值。

```
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$actual", actual);
```

以上代码会将 actual 的返回值添加到控制台中，这样我们可以很容易的观察到这个值。

## 3.1.8 自动生成预备条件

Pex 可以自动生成前置条件，这对于我们检查我们的测试是否正确运行非常有用。Pex 会自动生成函数/方法调用所需的前置条件，并且会在测试执行中检查这些条件。

例如有一个函数将值添加到列表中，Pex 将自动生成一个列表为 null 或超过最大大小的情况来检查这个函数。

```
[PexMethod]
[PexEntryPoint]
public List<string> AddItemToList(string item)
{
    // 前置条件
    if (item == null) throw new ArgumentNullException("item");
    Assume.IsNotNull(item, "item");

    // ...
    List<string> list = new List<string>();
    list.Add(item);

    // ... 
    return list;
}
```

## 3.1.9 强制约束和保证

当 Pex 生成测试用例时，我们可以通过假设和强制来确保某些条件得到满足。假设允许我们向 Pex 提供关于函数参数的常识信息。这可以帮助 Pex 有效地生成测试用例。

```
[PexMethod]
[PexEntryPoint]
public int Add(int a, int b)
{
    PexAssume.IsTrue(a >= 0 && b >= 0); // 假设 a 和 b 是正数
    int sum = a + b;
    PexAssert.IsTrue(sum >= a); // 保证和不会小于 a
    return sum;
}
```

如果函数的输入或输出违反了这些假设或保证，那么生成的测试用例将不会被执行，这使得我们的测试更具有鲁棒性。

## 3.1.10 自动生成测试覆盖率报告

Pex 可以自动生成测试覆盖率报告，这可以帮助我们了解我们的代码在哪些地方还没有被测试到。

```
Pex.Re--------------------	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that get index value is not out of range
	    // an assertion that the collection count hasn't changed after using the getter 
	    // an assertion that the collection contains the object after using the getter
        }
    }
}

Thanks in advance

A: The contracts specified for methods are not related to the properties of the list, but rather to the behavior of the method when given some inputs. In the case of your PUT method, the contract specifies the precondition that the provided index is within the range of valid indices for the ArrayList.
However, the ArrayList in .NET is essentially a wrapper around a T[] array (it inherits from Array and overrides some methods), internally it only knows how to use its own methods and properties, so its contract doesn't consider the T[] array. So according to the PexEngine, the contract of ArrayList.get_Item(index) is not satisfied because it doesn't know that the index should not be out of the range of a T[] array. 
If you want to add a contract to the getter property, you can try writing a custom contract solver, but it may be overkill and you may easily miss some things. And remember that the main purpose of PEX testing is not to solve contracts but to verify the functionality and behavior of the tests. The contracts are meant to help to understand the behavior of the methods under different conditions, rather than providing a 100% guarantee that the algorithm is correct.
To make sure the get operation is valid, you should consider testing it with valid inputs and postconditions. 
Please refer to this link for more details on contract testing: https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/dn646881.aspx
It is important to note that Pex does not guarantee that a test case satisfies a method's contracts - it only ensures that the method's behavior at these inputs is well-understood. Also be aware that Pex uses a model of your code to make decisions. When it can't determine a possible value, it uses default values instead.

A: In order to get around this issue, you should consider setting a precondition for your PUT method that ensures the index is within the bounds of the ArrayList. 
Something along the lines of:
[PexMethod]
[PexPrecondition(Method = "typeof(ArrayListContractTest).GetItemPrecondition")] 
public void PUT_Get(ArrayList arrList, int index) { ... }

Where GetItemPrecondition is a static method that handles the precondition:
using Microsoft.Practices.EnterpriseLibrary.Common.Utility;
...

public static bool GetItemPrecondition(int index, ArrayList arrList){
   Check.IsNotNull(arrList, "arrList"); 
   return index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count;
}

Notice the use of Check.IsNotNull to prevent a Null Reference Exception. The precondition must be a static method that returns a bool, the parameters are the inputs to the test, and any additional objects that could affect the outcome as null values of these inputs can cause the test to fail. In your case, the arrList parameter will be preconditioned by a null ArrayList - if this is not a possible state for your code, you should include a non-null assertion in GetItemPrecondition. 
This way you can make sure that Pex knows not to use the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute on your getter test as it already knows its inputs are valid thanks to the [PexPrecondition] attribute.
Please remember to always return something from the preconditions method, as for a failed precondition, Pex tests will be skipped.
Also remember to keep Pex happy by only testing a part of your code. Testing all methods from the API might make Pex bored by all the trivial operations and focus on the important things when possible.
Also, be aware that unit tests should test your code in isolation, so instead of relying on an external ArrayList, you should create your own methods that emulate the behavior of ArrayList to be able to accurately test your code.
Please try this approach, it should help you getting around the issue. 

