In order to get around this issue, you should consider setting a precondition for your PUT method that ensures the index is within the bounds of the ArrayList. This can be represented in the PexMethodAttribute. Preconditions specify the conditions that must be met for the execution of a method. 

This can be achieved with the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute. This tells Pex to apply the method as a precondition when testing--------------------            PexAssert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount, "Should maintain count");
            PexAssert.IsTrue(New_containsNewRet, "New element should be contained");

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that index value is within the boundaries of ArrayList.
            PexAssert.IsTrue((New_Ret == arrList[Old_index]), "Should maintain elements");
        }
    }
}

I already checked the PexAssumeUnderTest annotation on the first parameter of this test method. It's ArrayList type which is annotated as PexAssumeUnderTest. I cannot see what's wrong with my code.
Any advice or help is welcome. Thanks in advance.

A: You are using the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute on the second parameter, which makes the framework assume that it's under test. But looking at the actual code, you are only checking that the index is within bounds and using it as a condition for an assertion. The check that the index is within bounds isn't guaranteed, because you are using the AssumePrecondition.IsTrue method, which may or may not get run in some testing engine versions.
In your code, you should use the [PexAssumeValid] attribute for the second parameter. The [PexAssumeValid] attribute ensures that the testing engine assumes that all preconditions for the method are valid, and skips the current test if any of them fail. It guarantees that the framework skips the current test when these conditions are not met.
You can change 
public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
to
public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeValid]int index)
Or check if the index is in valid boundary using the following Assert.IsTrue:
PexAssert.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0, "Invalid index boundary");

A: The issue here is that index in this test is assumed valid by the fuzzer without checking if it is within the ArrayList bounds. You should adjust the second parameter to be [PexAssumeValid] to ensure that it valid for the testing framework to run it.
Also, you should add an assertion to make sure that index is within the ArrayList bounds.
This is your revised code: 
public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeValid]int index)
{
    // your code here...

    PexAssert.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0, "Index is out of bounds");

    // rest of your code...
}

Adding [PexAssumeValid] for index will ensure the tested method to behave uniformly. It informs the fuzzer about the range of values that are valid for testing at design time. And adding index assertion informs the fuzzer about the valid state of your method during testing.
But remember that the testing engine needs to run the test multiple times for different inputs to understand the behavior of your method, so it needs to be robust and exhaustive. If your method has some exception handling or pre-conditions, consider those in your fuzzing or testing activities.
Hope this helps, happy testing!!!

A: I ran my test, indeed it was giving an IndexOutOfRange exception as the input index was not in the range. 

I fixed the issue by removing the AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0); and replacing it with PexAssert.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0, "Index is out of bounds");. 
Then this test works fine.
Hope it helps to those who are encountering the same issue too.

But please note, you must ensure the bounds of your ArrayList always. Because ArrayList is a dynamically-sized array, you can easily add/remove items from it and thus its size can change arbitrarily. So, you should not rely on the size of ArrayList when testing its getter (operator[]) by index. If you really need the size always match some predefined value, you could introduce an other variable holding that size and ensure that it always returns the same.

Thank you, and happy testing.

A: It looks like your index is getting validated in the AssumePrecondition while PexAssumeValid should be used on parameters which are meant to be valid conditions in your method. The index in your code acts as a pre-condition of the method (since you are using arrList[index]), if PexAssumeValid is used on the index--------------------